rav1d_pin_66f58fa6 / 2026-08-29
Exposure measurement for the rav1d-safe dev-dep bump
a6a7e232f9cd -> 66f58fa6a64c (374 commits), taken BEFORE the pin landed.

WHY THE BUMP
  Three rav1d-safe git revs were live across the zen workspace: zenavif on
  66f58fa6 (current main), this repo on a6a7e232 (2026-08-06), zenmetrics
  resolving 140f9145 (2026-08-11). All three older revs predate
  rav1d-safe@3426ebf7 (rav1d-safe#524), an intermittent DisjointMut overlap
  panic in the 8bpc x86_64 loop-filter H kernels. Aligning on the rev zenavif
  settled on means the workspace decodes AVIF through one decoder.

WHAT USES rav1d-safe HERE
  Nothing in the shipped crate. It is a dev-dependency of the root `cavif`
  package, consumed by two examples that decode the encoder's own output:
      examples/gate_sweep.rs       release-gate A/B harness (bytes + ssim2)
      examples/hdr_pq10_probe.rs   10-bit PQ round-trip probe
  Both build their decoder from `Settings::default()`; neither calls
  `Decoder::flush()`. The `ctx.flush()` calls in ravif/src/{animated,
  av1encoder}.rs are zenrav1e's ENCODER context, an unrelated method — so
  rav1d-safe@59eb17b (flush drains owed frames before it resets) has no
  effect on this repo.
  NOTE: no CI job compiles rav1d-safe. Every job in .github/workflows/ci.yml
  is `-p zenravif`, and the pin lives in the root package. CI resolves it,
  never builds it. Everything below is LOCAL aarch64 evidence; the x86_64
  legs of the range are not covered by any measurement in this repo.

INSTRUMENT
  examples/gate_sweep, dev profile (opt-level 1, deps opt-level 2), threads=1.
  One row per (source, class, size, speed, quality) cell:
  bytes / bpp are the encoder's; sha256 is over the encoded AVIF; ssim2 is
  SSIMULACRA2 of the rav1d-safe decode against the resampled source. So a
  single run separates "the encoder moved" (bytes/sha256) from "the decoder
  moved" (ssim2 with bytes held fixed).

GRID  (1200 cells per arm; workspace sweep discipline: four size tiers, q
       density equal at low and high q, five content classes, six speeds)
  A   sizes 64,256,1024,2048  speeds 10        q 5..100 step 5   400 cells
  B   sizes 64,256            speeds 4,6,8     q 5..100 step 5   600 cells
  C   size  64                speeds 1,2       q 5..100 step 5   200 cells
  sources: clic2025 photo x2, gb82-sc screen / text_ui / lineart_ui
  1099 distinct encoded streams across the 1200 cells.

ARMS  (label column)
  old    a6a7e232f9cd   the pin this repo was on
  mid    140f9145 0c35  grid A only — the bisect midpoint (what zenmetrics
                        resolved, and what zenavif ran from 08-11 to 08-29)
  new    66f58fa6a64c   the pin landed by this commit
  new2   66f58fa6a64c   grid A only — a REPEAT of `new`, the determinism
                        control that makes the comparison meaningful

RESULT 1 — determinism control
  new vs new2, 400 cells: 0 rows differ in any column but enc_ms.
  The harness is deterministic, so every difference below is real.

RESULT 2 — Settings::strictness now defaults to Strict (rav1d-safe@2e0f7e8)
  Non-conforming streams return Error::InvalidData instead of being
  concealed. `Settings` is #[non_exhaustive], so this is a behaviour change,
  not an API break, and both examples take the new default.
  ACCEPT/REJECT, 1200 cells each arm:
      old  1200 accepted / 0 rejected     (0 DECFAIL, 0 ENCFAIL, 0 NaN ssim2)
      new  1200 accepted / 0 rejected     (0 DECFAIL, 0 ENCFAIL, 0 NaN ssim2)
  Zero newly-rejected streams. Recommendation: keep Strict. The corpus these
  harnesses decode is zenravif's OWN output, and a stream zenravif emits that
  libaom would reject is a zenravif bug the harness should surface rather
  than conceal — rav1d-safe#422 was found exactly that way, on a zenrav1e
  chroma-TU desync that passed an encode->decode round trip. Lenient stays
  available if a future harness needs to score deliberately-corrupt input;
  nothing here does.

RESULT 3 — encoder output is untouched
  bytes, bpp and sha256 are identical on all 1200 cells across all arms.
  0 differing rows in each of those three columns. As expected: nothing in
  the range touches the encode side.

RESULT 4 — decoded pixels DO move on aarch64, and the old side was wrong
  ssim2, old vs new, 1200 cells: 699 differ (58.2%),
      mean |delta| 0.039, max |delta| 0.610,
      402 higher / 297 lower, mean signed delta +0.0024
  — a rounding-shaped change, not a quality trend.

  Bisected on grid A (400 cells):
      a6a7e232 -> 140f9145  (278 commits)   339/400 differ (84.8%),
                                            mean |d| 0.031, max |d| 0.334
      140f9145 -> 66f58fa6  ( 96 commits)     0/400 differ
      a6a7e232 -> 66f58fa6  (374 commits)   339/400 differ  (identical to the
                                            first row — the whole movement is
                                            in the first sub-range)

  So the 96-commit range zenavif took on 2026-08-29 is bit-neutral here too,
  independently confirmed on aarch64. The movement is entirely the aarch64
  NEON conformance campaign of 2026-08-07/08, which this repo had not taken
  and which zenavif/zenmetrics absorbed on 2026-08-11:
      rav1d-safe@ef01e85  itx: the 16x16 per-type eob thresholds (dav1d gives
                          H_DCT/V_DCT eob_half 8, not 36 — for eob in [8,36)
                          the kernel skipped rows 8..15 that had real
                          coefficients) and the 16x32/32x16 rect2 roundings
                          the identity path collapsed to one shift.   +293
      rav1d-safe@ddbe8ba  mc: the 16bpc compound seam ran TWO PREP_BIAS
                          conventions at once, and intermediate_bits was a
                          literal 4 instead of 14 - bitdepth.          + 91
      8bpc compound (avg/w_avg/mask/w_mask)                            + 80

  Upstream's set-diff-by-name record — rav1d-safe
  benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07.meta, against dav1d's published
  MD5s over the 766 dav1d-test-data vectors — is
      before  302 PASS / 464 MISMATCH
      after   766 PASS /   0 MISMATCH
  with 0 newly-failing at every intermediate step.

  a6a7e232 is therefore the WRONG side on aarch64: it decoded 464 of 766
  dav1d conformance vectors to the wrong pixels. The ssim2 movement measured
  here is this repo's harness moving off a broken decoder, not onto one.

  x86_64 across the same range was NOT MEASURED — this host is aarch64 and
  cannot run those kernels. Three things on the record make an x86_64 shift
  unlikely, and they are inferences, not local results: the campaign's
  arithmetic fixes live in `_arm` kernels plus a few dispatcher lines
  (src/itx.rs, src/mc.rs); the 302/766 baseline is explicitly the aarch64 one
  (the record is titled "aarch64 MD5 conformance"); and rav1d-safe@04fafc2,
  inside this range, measured x86_64 at 766 PASS / 0 MISMATCH with the
  set-diff on (name, status, actual_md5) empty across x86 t=1, x86 t=8,
  aarch64 t=1 and aarch64 t=8 — identical pixels on both architectures at the
  END of the range. Note the range DOES touch non-_arm safe_simd files
  (cdef.rs, ipred.rs, loopfilter.rs, itx/part10_dispatch.rs, mod.rs); those
  are the guard/reservation and dispatch changes of the #455/#494 rounds, not
  kernel arithmetic, but this repo has not verified that claim by measurement.

RESULT 5 — the 10-bit path does NOT move, across the WHOLE range
  examples/hdr_pq10_probe (64x48 PQ10, Planes::Depth16) prints the same line at
  a6a7e232 and at 66f58fa6:
      q95 s8: max |D| = 1281, mean |D| = 47 (16-bit units), 1127 bytes
  So the movement in RESULT 4 is the 8bpc intra path specifically, not the
  decoder wholesale. That is consistent with what the campaign contains:
  rav1d-safe@ddbe8ba's 16bpc repair is in the compound/prep MC kernels
  (inter prediction), which an intra-only still never reaches, while
  @ef01e85's itx eob/rect2 defects sit on the transform path a still DOES
  use. It also says the 10/12-bit NEON inverse transforms ADDED in the range
  (rav1d-safe@560f4ec) are bit-exact on this fixture rather than merely close.
  One fixture, one size, one quality — narrow, but it is a measurement, and it
  bounds the blast radius of the bump to 8bpc.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE EXISTING GATE-FLIP RECORD
  benchmarks/gate_flip_summary_2026-08-06.tsv.meta names
  `rav1d-safe a6a7e232f9cd (decode side only)` and was run on this same
  Apple M4 Pro. Its ssim2 numbers were therefore scored through the
  pre-campaign aarch64 decoder. The bytes/BD-rate half of that record is
  unaffected (encoder output is untouched, RESULT 3), but its ssim2 column
  must NOT be compared cell-for-cell against any post-bump run. Re-measure a
  baseline on this pin before the next gate flip.

